From 3c5253c09d2299dab051610a7a2ca883c636c2c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:31:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GBM: the black of the display for Dolby Vision Low-Latency,
 set by hand (videoscreen.hadesdvllblack)

Low-Latency could be mapped to a peak and a gamut set by hand
(videoscreen.hadesdvllmanual), for a display that says more or less
than it does, but its black stayed that of the source. A projector or
an LCD has a black well above it, and the shadows just above black sink
into it.

"Low-Latency: black of the display" (videoscreen.hadesdvllblack, under
the peak and colours set by hand, expert level): 0 to 0.5 cd/m2 in steps
of 0.0005, 0 by default, which keeps the black of the source as before.
TakeDVLowLatencyMapping() gives it to the mapping (dvbridge_hdr10_mapping
::black: the black level lift of ITU-R BT.2390 in the Low-Latency hook)
and the line of kodi.log that gives the target says it.

Measured on the software bridge, four P7 FEL pictures at 800 cd/m2 in
DCI-P3, luma of the mapped picture against black 0: with 0.05 cd/m2
the darkest parts (0 to 0.0015 cd/m2) rise by 184 codes of 4095 on
average, to 0.058 cd/m2; 0.27 cd/m2 goes to 0.54, 2.0 to 2.5, 22 to 23;
above 92 cd/m2 the luma moves by 1.8 codes on average, 5.3 at most; with
0.0005 cd/m2 the darkest parts rise by 17 codes, to 0.0017 cd/m2, and
above 92 cd/m2 by 0.17. The help says it in plain words. English and
Spanish.
